1
00:00:00,040 --> 00:00:01,920
AI stocks were getting hit.

2
00:00:01,920 --> 00:00:05,269
So how did equal weight stocks and small caps finish

3
00:00:05,269 --> 00:00:06,360
the week in green?

4
00:00:06,360 --> 00:00:10,706
The answer matters before next week's Fed decision, inflation data

5
00:00:10,706 --> 00:00:12,760
and giant technology earnings.

6
00:00:12,760 --> 00:00:16,056
In this review, we'll separate the index from the stocks

7
00:00:16,056 --> 00:00:18,979
inside it, unpack oil and the new cyber threat, and

8
00:00:18,979 --> 00:00:21,725
build a calm checklist for what comes next.

9
00:00:21,915 --> 00:00:28,475
For the week ending October 23, SPY fell 0.43% and

10
00:00:28,475 --> 00:00:30,115
QQQ lost 0.76%.

11
00:00:30,115 --> 00:00:32,915
But the Dow gained 0.41%.

12
00:00:32,915 --> 00:00:38,661
The equal weight S&P fund, RSP, rose 1.14%, and small

13
00:00:38,661 --> 00:00:43,555
caps, represented by IWM, gained 1.96%.

14
00:00:43,555 --> 00:00:47,186
These are Friday to Friday price changes, not just Friday's

15
00:00:47,186 --> 00:00:47,850
session.

16
00:00:48,040 --> 00:00:50,320
Why can those numbers disagree?

17
00:00:50,320 --> 00:00:55,320
A capitalization weighted index gives larger companies more influence.

18
00:00:55,320 --> 00:00:58,440
Equal weighting spreads influence more evenly.

19
00:00:58,440 --> 00:01:02,100
Imagine a classroom average where a few students count many

20
00:01:02,100 --> 00:01:03,980
times more than everyone else.

21
00:01:03,980 --> 00:01:07,656
If those students struggle, the average can fall even while

22
00:01:07,656 --> 00:01:09,040
many others improve.

23
00:01:09,040 --> 00:01:12,545
That is an illustration, not a calculation of this week's

24
00:01:12,545 --> 00:01:14,682
exact index contribution.

25
00:01:14,682 --> 00:01:19,425
The gap between SPY and RSP supports a rotation story,

26
00:01:19,425 --> 00:01:22,767
not proof that every smaller company was healthy.

27
00:01:22,957 --> 00:01:27,604
Eight of the eleven major S&P sector ETFs finished the

28
00:01:27,604 --> 00:01:28,637
week higher.

29
00:01:28,637 --> 00:01:34,237
Energy gained about 1.6% and healthcare about 1.45%.

30
00:01:34,237 --> 00:01:39,417
Financials, utilities and real estate were also positive.

31
00:01:39,417 --> 00:01:45,757
Communication services fell 4.36%, while industrials lost 1.87%.

32
00:01:45,757 --> 00:01:49,364
This is why a single technology headline can give an

33
00:01:49,364 --> 00:01:50,757
incomplete picture.

34
00:01:50,757 --> 00:01:54,537
Sector funds are useful comparison tools, but they are not

35
00:01:54,537 --> 00:01:56,725
a count of every advancing stock.

36
00:01:56,915 --> 00:02:00,728
There were severe losses in parts of the AI trade.

37
00:02:00,728 --> 00:02:08,035
CoreWeave fell about 11.65%, Meta lost 9.40% and Iren fell

38
00:02:08,035 --> 00:02:09,195
7.73%.

39
00:02:09,195 --> 00:02:13,715
The semiconductor fund SOXX lost 3.08%.

40
00:02:13,715 --> 00:02:17,435
Yet Palantir rose 5.76%.

41
00:02:17,435 --> 00:02:21,765
So the honest description is selective damage and uneven leadership,

42
00:02:21,765 --> 00:02:24,405
not that every AI company collapsed.

43
00:02:24,405 --> 00:02:28,520
These examples illustrate the dispersion; they are not the entire

44
00:02:28,520 --> 00:02:29,017
sector.

45
00:02:29,207 --> 00:02:31,827
The weekly return also hides the path.

46
00:02:31,827 --> 00:02:37,299
QQQ recovered to $725.20 on Wednesday, then fell to $704.47

47
00:02:37,299 --> 00:02:38,587
on Thursday.

48
00:02:38,587 --> 00:02:42,427
That was a drop of about 2.86% in one session.

49
00:02:42,427 --> 00:02:45,887
Friday was almost unchanged for QQQ.

50
00:02:45,887 --> 00:02:51,047
Reuters reported that Meta's results reignited concern about AI spending.

51
00:02:51,047 --> 00:02:54,508
The sharp technology shock was concentrated late in the week,

52
00:02:54,508 --> 00:02:56,225
after an earlier recovery.

53
00:02:56,415 --> 00:03:00,232
The ten year Treasury yield finished near 4.99%, about eleven

54
00:03:00,232 --> 00:03:03,735
basis points below the previous Friday.

55
00:03:03,735 --> 00:03:07,256
Lower yields can support valuations, but falling yields can also

56
00:03:07,256 --> 00:03:11,335
reflect concern about growth or demand for safer assets.

57
00:03:11,335 --> 00:03:15,295
Meanwhile, rising oil can create inflation pressure.

58
00:03:15,295 --> 00:03:18,095
Those forces can pull in opposite directions.

59
00:03:18,095 --> 00:03:20,904
This week's bond move is another reason not to describe

60
00:03:20,904 --> 00:03:24,063
the market with a single risk on or risk off

61
00:03:24,063 --> 00:03:24,433
label.

62
00:03:24,623 --> 00:03:27,183
Here is another apparent contradiction.

63
00:03:27,183 --> 00:03:33,903
VIX ended at 19.45, below the previous Friday's 19.99.

64
00:03:33,903 --> 00:03:37,663
But it had fallen to 16.68 by Wednesday.

65
00:03:37,663 --> 00:03:42,263
From Wednesday to Friday, it climbed about 16.6%.

66
00:03:42,263 --> 00:03:46,380
Both statements are true: lower over the full week, sharply

67
00:03:46,380 --> 00:03:48,343
higher from the midweek low.

68
00:03:48,343 --> 00:03:52,153
Always check the starting point before reacting to a percentage

69
00:03:52,153 --> 00:03:52,993
in a headline.

70
00:03:52,993 --> 00:03:58,073
VIX describes expected volatility, not a guaranteed direction.

71
00:03:58,073 --> 00:04:00,016
No single reading proves a bottom.

72
00:04:00,207 --> 00:04:02,707
Oil was the other major pressure point.

73
00:04:02,707 --> 00:04:08,587
Reuters reported Friday settlements of $103.27 for West Texas crude

74
00:04:08,587 --> 00:04:12,267
and $116.72 for Brent.

75
00:04:12,267 --> 00:04:15,227
Brent finished the week about six percent higher.

76
00:04:15,227 --> 00:04:19,588
These are futures prices, not the returns of an oil

77
00:04:19,588 --> 00:04:19,907
ETF.

78
00:04:19,907 --> 00:04:24,067
Oil funds can behave differently because of contracts, rolling and

79
00:04:24,067 --> 00:04:25,057
fees.

80
00:04:25,057 --> 00:04:28,437
For the broad market, the concern is what sustained energy

81
00:04:28,437 --> 00:04:31,475
costs could do to inflation and company margins.

82
00:04:31,665 --> 00:04:36,518
Reuters linked the supply concern to Iran aligned Houthi attacks

83
00:04:36,518 --> 00:04:37,665
in Saudi Arabia.

84
00:04:37,665 --> 00:04:41,745
Authorities in the United Arab Emirates also reported an oil

85
00:04:41,745 --> 00:04:45,585
spill, but its cause was not identified in that report.

86
00:04:45,585 --> 00:04:49,425
Don't merge separate incidents into one confirmed story.

87
00:04:49,425 --> 00:04:53,655
For Iran, the United States and Israel, watch verified statements,

88
00:04:53,655 --> 00:04:56,665
shipping conditions and actual supply disruptions.

89
00:04:56,665 --> 00:05:01,488
Escalation could keep pressure on oil; credible de escalation could

90
00:05:01,488 --> 00:05:02,165
reduce it.

91
00:05:02,165 --> 00:05:05,392
Neither outcome is established in advance.

92
00:05:05,582 --> 00:05:08,822
The transmission is simple, but not automatic.

93
00:05:08,822 --> 00:05:13,927
Higher energy costs can squeeze transport and manufacturing margins, affect

94
00:05:13,927 --> 00:05:17,902
household spending, and complicate the inflation outlook.

95
00:05:17,902 --> 00:05:22,205
Energy producers may benefit from higher selling prices, while other

96
00:05:22,205 --> 00:05:24,062
businesses face higher costs.

97
00:05:24,062 --> 00:05:27,780
That helps explain why energy can rise while the broad

98
00:05:27,780 --> 00:05:28,702
index falls.

99
00:05:28,702 --> 00:05:31,714
How long the shock lasts matters more than a single

100
00:05:31,714 --> 00:05:35,937
dramatic headline, and companies differ in their hedging, costs and

101
00:05:35,937 --> 00:05:37,767
ability to pass prices on.

102
00:05:37,957 --> 00:05:42,797
A separate story worth understanding is PHP2Shell.

103
00:05:42,797 --> 00:05:47,837
The Dutch National Cyber Security Centre documented two critical vulnerabilities

104
00:05:47,837 --> 00:05:51,517
and emergency fixes published on October 21.

105
00:05:51,517 --> 00:05:55,517
Affected web servers can be exposed to remote code execution,

106
00:05:55,517 --> 00:05:58,445
meaning an attacker may be able to run code on

107
00:05:58,445 --> 00:05:59,157
the server.

108
00:05:59,157 --> 00:06:02,388
But a vulnerability is not proof that every website was

109
00:06:02,388 --> 00:06:02,957
breached.

110
00:06:02,957 --> 00:06:08,064
Exposure, successful intrusion and confirmed data theft are different claims,

111
00:06:08,064 --> 00:06:10,517
and they require different evidence.

112
00:06:10,707 --> 00:06:13,987
Cybersecurity shares were a striking contrast.

113
00:06:13,987 --> 00:06:20,609
CrowdStrike gained about 13.27% for the week, Palo Alto Networks

114
00:06:20,609 --> 00:06:24,307
11.27% and Cloudflare 9.93%.

115
00:06:24,307 --> 00:06:29,387
News coverage discussed the security threat and improving spending expectations.

116
00:06:29,387 --> 00:06:34,057
Those are possible contributors, not proof of one single cause.

117
00:06:34,057 --> 00:06:38,596
The investing question is whether demand turns into durable revenue

118
00:06:38,596 --> 00:06:39,457
and cash flow.

119
00:06:39,457 --> 00:06:42,892
A large share price jump alone cannot answer that.

120
00:06:43,082 --> 00:06:46,122
Now to October 26–30.

121
00:06:46,122 --> 00:06:49,781
The Fed meets Tuesday and Wednesday, with its decision on

122
00:06:49,781 --> 00:06:50,482
Wednesday.

123
00:06:50,482 --> 00:06:54,682
The Bureau of Economic Analysis schedules both the advance third

124
00:06:54,682 --> 00:06:59,673
quarter GDP report and September personal income and outlays, which

125
00:06:59,673 --> 00:07:05,802
includes PCE inflation, for Thursday, October 29, at 8:30 ET.

126
00:07:05,802 --> 00:07:07,882
Both reports are on Thursday.

127
00:07:07,882 --> 00:07:11,352
They arrive after the Fed decision, so they can change

128
00:07:11,352 --> 00:07:15,517
expectations for future policy, not the decision already announced.

129
00:07:15,707 --> 00:07:19,131
For the Fed, the statement and the explanation can matter

130
00:07:19,131 --> 00:07:20,807
as much as the rate decision.

131
00:07:20,807 --> 00:07:25,522
Listen for how policymakers balance inflation, employment and growth, and

132
00:07:25,522 --> 00:07:28,087
how they discuss energy risks.

133
00:07:28,087 --> 00:07:32,299
Don't assume that holding rates steady is automatically friendly or

134
00:07:32,299 --> 00:07:33,407
hostile to stocks.

135
00:07:33,407 --> 00:07:37,488
The reaction depends partly on what investors expected beforehand.

136
00:07:37,488 --> 00:07:41,959
October is not a scheduled economic projections meeting, so don't

137
00:07:41,959 --> 00:07:45,358
build your plan around receiving a new official dot plot

138
00:07:45,358 --> 00:07:46,142
this week.

139
00:07:46,332 --> 00:07:49,452
GDP measures economic activity.

140
00:07:49,452 --> 00:07:53,288
The advance estimate can be revised, and the headline growth

141
00:07:53,288 --> 00:07:54,532
rate is annualized.

142
00:07:54,532 --> 00:07:59,075
PCE asks a different question: what is happening to consumer

143
00:07:59,075 --> 00:07:59,532
prices?

144
00:07:59,532 --> 00:08:03,751
Core PCE excludes food and energy directly, so an oil

145
00:08:03,751 --> 00:08:07,492
spike does not mechanically appear in that month's core number.

146
00:08:07,492 --> 00:08:10,132
Indirect cost effects can take time.

147
00:08:10,132 --> 00:08:14,962
Read the monthly pace, annual trend, spending details and revisions

148
00:08:14,962 --> 00:08:15,652
together.

149
00:08:15,652 --> 00:08:20,263
Strong growth with persistent inflation creates a different policy discussion

150
00:08:20,263 --> 00:08:22,809
from weaker growth with cooling prices.

151
00:08:22,998 --> 00:08:27,158
The earnings calendar lists Microsoft and Alphabet after Wednesday's close,

152
00:08:27,158 --> 00:08:30,398
followed by Amazon after Thursday's close.

153
00:08:30,398 --> 00:08:34,114
That puts three major cloud and AI spending stories beside

154
00:08:34,114 --> 00:08:35,318
the macro calendar.

155
00:08:35,318 --> 00:08:38,598
For Microsoft, watch Azure and margins.

156
00:08:38,598 --> 00:08:42,718
For Alphabet, watch search, cloud and capital spending.

157
00:08:42,718 --> 00:08:47,998
For Amazon, watch AWS, retail profitability and investment plans.

158
00:08:47,998 --> 00:08:51,998
These companies can move major indexes because of their size.

159
00:08:51,998 --> 00:08:57,089
Expectations and guidance matter alongside the reported quarter, and scheduled

160
00:08:57,089 --> 00:08:59,433
release times can change.

161
00:08:59,623 --> 00:09:02,073
The wider calendar matters too.

162
00:09:02,073 --> 00:09:05,996
Coca Cola and SoFi are listed for Tuesday morning, with

163
00:09:05,996 --> 00:09:07,583
Visa after the close.

164
00:09:07,583 --> 00:09:11,607
Thursday includes Caterpillar and Eli Lilly before the open, and

165
00:09:11,607 --> 00:09:13,343
Intel after the close.

166
00:09:13,343 --> 00:09:16,543
Exxon and Chevron are listed for Friday morning.

167
00:09:16,543 --> 00:09:22,194
Together they offer clues about consumers, industrial activity, healthcare, chips

168
00:09:22,194 --> 00:09:23,323
and energy.

169
00:09:23,323 --> 00:09:27,363
This is a selected watchlist, not every company reporting.

170
00:09:27,363 --> 00:09:31,515
Check each company's investor relations page before relying on an

171
00:09:31,515 --> 00:09:32,850
exact release time.

172
00:09:33,040 --> 00:09:38,263
On the chart, QQQ finished at $704.40, near this week's

173
00:09:38,263 --> 00:09:39,480
low of $701.16.

174
00:09:39,480 --> 00:09:43,744
Its fifty day average is near $725.81, with the twenty

175
00:09:43,744 --> 00:09:45,480
day average higher still.

176
00:09:45,480 --> 00:09:49,378
Holding the weekly low and eventually reclaiming those averages would

177
00:09:49,378 --> 00:09:51,040
improve the recovery case.

178
00:09:51,040 --> 00:09:55,049
Losing the low while volatility rises would strengthen the caution

179
00:09:55,049 --> 00:09:55,590
case.

180
00:09:55,590 --> 00:09:59,808
These are reference levels and conditional scenarios, not targets or

181
00:09:59,808 --> 00:10:01,350
instructions to trade.

182
00:10:01,540 --> 00:10:05,980
For SPY, the weekly low is $758.68.

183
00:10:05,980 --> 00:10:10,380
Its fifty day average is around $767.81.

184
00:10:10,380 --> 00:10:14,388
The constructive case needs more than one green candle: price

185
00:10:14,388 --> 00:10:19,820
stabilizing, broader participation staying healthy, and volatility settling.

186
00:10:19,820 --> 00:10:22,559
The weaker case is a break of support while the

187
00:10:22,559 --> 00:10:25,590
equal weight market also starts losing strength.

188
00:10:25,590 --> 00:10:29,543
Next week's earnings, oil and macro news could change either

189
00:10:29,543 --> 00:10:30,430
case quickly.

190
00:10:30,430 --> 00:10:33,100
A gap can jump straight through a chart level.

191
00:10:33,290 --> 00:10:36,832
Before the next session, write down the event, the expectation

192
00:10:36,832 --> 00:10:38,290
and the market's response.

193
00:10:38,290 --> 00:10:41,284
Check whether a price is current, whether a percentage is

194
00:10:41,284 --> 00:10:44,303
daily or weekly, and whether a headline comes from a

195
00:10:44,303 --> 00:10:46,070
source that can be verified.

196
00:10:46,070 --> 00:10:49,930
Then compare price, breadth, volatility and oil.

197
00:10:49,930 --> 00:10:52,850
You don't have to react to every notification.

198
00:10:52,850 --> 00:10:56,106
Waiting for a clearer picture is a valid choice, especially

199
00:10:56,106 --> 00:10:58,767
when several large events arrive close together.

200
00:10:58,957 --> 00:11:01,637
This week was not one uniform collapse.

201
00:11:01,637 --> 00:11:04,540
It was a split market, with real damage in parts

202
00:11:04,540 --> 00:11:08,957
of AI, stronger participation elsewhere, rising oil and a busy

203
00:11:08,957 --> 00:11:09,837
week ahead.

204
00:11:09,837 --> 00:11:13,388
Follow Market Mind Trading Basics for the next breakdown, and

205
00:11:13,388 --> 00:11:16,237
tell us which report you want explained next.

